00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:56.860
But you guys have mentioned in previous podcasts that Oscar was the only one to really have sound on his computer.
确实如此，弗兰基。

665
00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:58.460
So true, Frankie.
那么，这是怎么做到的呢？

666
00:25:58.700 --> 00:25:59.920
So, how did this work?
嗯，兰迪说所有的电脑播放视频时都没有声音，他们实际上是在后期的制作中才把声音加进去的。

667
00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:08.940
Well, Randy said all of the computers ran the videos without sound, and they actually added that in later in post-production.
所以，我们是在假装对声音做出反应。

668
00:26:09.260 --> 00:26:11.400
So, we're fakie reacting to sound.
没错。

669
00:26:11.620 --> 00:26:11.860
Yep.
嗯哼。

670
00:26:12.140 --> 00:26:12.500
Mm-hmm.
正是如此。

671
00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:13.080
Exactly.
真实的视频，虚假的声音。

672
00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:15.300
Real video, fakie sound.
最后，兰迪说小水獭的视频是来自加州蒙特雷水族馆的素材。

673
00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:24.500
And finally, Randy said that the video of the baby otters was footage from the Monterey, California, Aquarium.
但是，你知道，水獭最终并没有做出迈克尔在最后采访片段中描述的任何动作。

674
00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:31.280
But, you know, the otters do not end up doing any of the things that Michael describes in his final talking head.
这就是为什么你没看到太多视频内容，因为它们对不上。

675
00:26:31.700 --> 00:26:35.440
That's why you don't see too much of the video, because it wouldn't match.
视频只是在迈克尔的脸上呈现出来。

676
00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:38.280
It just kind of plays out on Michael's face.
是啊。

677
00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:38.820
Yeah.
以一种最令人愉悦的方式。

678
00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:40.240
In the most delightful way.
真的，你会完全相信它。

679
00:26:40.340 --> 00:26:41.300
Like, you really believe it.
没错，你会信的。

680
00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:42.100
Yeah, you do.
你准备好迎接一个最令人惊叹、最引人注目的场景了吗？有人一边说话，一边剪着报纸。

681
00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:50.120
Are you ready for one of the most amazing, commanding scenes of someone talking and just cutting up a piece of newspaper?
令人着迷？

682
00:26:51.380 --> 00:26:51.900
Mesmerizing?
我连续看了三遍。

683
00:26:52.240 --> 00:26:53.960
I watched it three times in a row.
我写了“令人着迷”。

684
00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:54.980
I wrote mesmerizing.
看我写了什么。

685
00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:55.840
Look what I wrote.
我被那个场景迷住了。

686
00:26:55.940 --> 00:26:58.320
I was mesmerized by the scene.
大家伙儿，我正在给珍娜看我的纸。

687
00:26:58.460 --> 00:26:59.880
I'm showing Jenna my paper, you guys.
乔·贝内特要来了，她来势汹汹。

688
00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:03.720
Joe Bennett is going to arrive, and she is coming in hot.
哦，没错。

689
00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:05.280
Oh, yeah.
是啊。

690
00:27:05.420 --> 00:27:05.680
Yeah.
她带着两个律师和她的狗进来了。

691
00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:09.120
She comes in with two lawyers and her dogs.
嗯哼。

692
00:27:09.340 --> 00:27:09.740
Mm-hmm.
这是一次突击访问。

693
00:27:09.900 --> 00:27:11.400
It's a surprise visit.
没人预料到她会来。

694
00:27:11.400 --> 00:27:12.900
No one was expecting her.
她接着解释说，报纸刊登了一篇关于赛博公司廉价的外国打印机攻击无辜美国人的报道。

695
00:27:12.900 --> 00:27:22.620
And she goes on to explain that the newspaper has run a story about Saber's cheap foreign printers attacking innocent Americans.
当她说这些话的时候，她在房间里走来走去。

696
00:27:22.620 --> 00:27:26.420
And while she's saying all of this, she's moving around the room.
嗯哼。

697
00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:26.840
Mm-hmm.
她拿了一把剪刀。

698
00:27:26.900 --> 00:27:28.140
She's getting a pair of scissors.
她在剪那篇报道。

699
00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:30.220
She's cutting the article.
当她进行这段对话时，我最喜欢的时刻之一是凯文说：

700
00:27:30.260 --> 00:27:33.940
As she has this dialogue, one of my favorite moments is when Kevin was like,
“是的，确实如此，玛妮。”

701
00:27:33.940 --> 00:27:35.500
I think I know what happened.
我想我知道发生了什么。

702
00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:37.440
And she goes, I don't think you do.
她说，我觉得你不知道。

703
00:27:37.700 --> 00:27:40.180
And then he goes, no, I'm thinking I don't.
然后他说，不，我想我确实不知道。

704
00:27:40.340 --> 00:27:40.840
I know.
我知道。

705
00:27:40.920 --> 00:27:41.920
I loved that, too.
我也很喜欢那段。

706
00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:46.140
But she's doing so much action while having all of this different emotion.
但她在表现出所有这些不同情绪的同时，还有那么多动作戏。

707
00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:53.680
It reminded me of that scene in Secret Santa when Michael had to go from Santa to Jesus all in one moment with his clothing.
这让我想起了《秘密圣诞老人》里的那一幕，迈克尔必须在瞬间通过服装从圣诞老人变身为耶稣。

708
00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:56.480
Well, we had a fan question from Anne S.
好吧，我们有一个来自瑞士苏黎世安妮·S的粉丝提问，她说，

709
00:27:56.680 --> 00:27:59.200
in Zurich, Switzerland, who said,
她说，

710
00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:05.860
can you explain Joe's insane choice to walk all the way over to Angela's desk to grab a glue stick,
你能解释一下乔为什么要做出那种疯狂的选择，大老远走到安吉拉的桌子前去拿一根胶棒，

711
00:28:06.060 --> 00:28:10.880
to glue the newspaper article to the picture of the office instead of using tape?
而不是用胶带把那篇报纸文章贴在办公室的照片上吗？

712
00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:19.920
I looked in the script, and all of this stage direction is meticulously outlined within the dialogue.
我查了剧本，所有这些舞台指示都在对话中被详细地列了出来。

713
00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:26.100
There is like this whole silent play that is scripted out, and I thought I would read it.
剧本里写了一整段默剧表演，我想我把它读出来。

714
00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:27.240
Oh, great.
哦，太好了。

715
00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:31.200
And then I have a little tidbit about when she comes to my desk.
然后关于她走到我桌子前的那一段，我还有一个小花絮。

716
00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:32.900
Ooh, all right.
噢，好的。

717
00:28:33.220 --> 00:28:34.640
Here's what it said in the script.
剧本里是这样写的。

718
00:28:34.800 --> 00:28:37.580
Now, mind you, within all of this, there's also dialogue.
请注意，在所有这些动作中，还有对话。

719
00:28:37.880 --> 00:28:38.040
Right.
没错。

720
00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:39.600
You're just reading the stage directions.
你只是在读舞台指示。

721
00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:39.980
Yes.
是的。

722
00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:43.520
Joe enters with a couple of well-dressed lawyers.
乔带着几个衣着考究的律师进来了。

723
00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:45.260
People are surprised.
大家都很惊讶。

724
00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:51.380
Joe pulls out a copy of the Times-Tribune off Jim's desk and casually flips through it.
乔从吉姆的桌子上拿了一份《时代论坛报》，漫不经心地翻阅着。

725
00:28:51.800 --> 00:28:56.100
Joe points to an article titled, Saber Defends Printers.
乔指着一篇题为《赛博捍卫打印机》的文章。

726
00:28:56.720 --> 00:29:01.420
As Joe speaks, she walks over to Dwight's desk and grabs a pair of scissors.
乔一边说话，一边走到德怀特的桌子前，拿了一把剪刀。

727
00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:04.480
Dwight looks like he might say something but swallows.
德怀特看起来想说点什么，但又咽了回去。

728
00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:07.520
Joe methodically cuts out the article.
乔有条不紊地剪下了那篇文章。

729
00:29:07.820 --> 00:29:09.820
Some people nervously chuckle.
有些人紧张地轻笑。

730
00:29:09.820 --> 00:29:15.840
Joe slowly carries the article toward accounting, dropping the scissors on Jim's desk as she walks.
乔慢慢地带着文章走向会计部，走的时候把剪刀扔在了吉姆的桌子上。

731
00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:18.140
Dwight is about to grab his scissors.
德怀特正要去拿他的剪刀。

732
00:29:18.380 --> 00:29:20.240
Jim pulls them just out of his reach.
吉姆把剪刀拉开，让他够不着。

733
00:29:20.480 --> 00:29:21.220
Dwight is furious.
德怀特气坏了。

734
00:29:22.040 --> 00:29:27.140
Joe starts rummaging over the tops of Oscar, Angela, and Kevin's desk looking for something.
乔开始在奥斯卡、安吉拉和凯文的桌子上翻找东西。

735
00:29:27.140 --> 00:29:31.140
Finally, she finds it on Angela's desk, a glue stick.
最后，她在安吉拉的桌子上找到了它，一根胶棒。

736
00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:37.380
Joe walks in a circle toward Michael's office while rubbing glue on the back of the article.
乔一边往迈克尔办公室的方向绕圈走，一边在文章背面涂胶水。

737
00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:40.220
Joe slams the article onto Pam's painting.
乔把文章猛地拍在帕姆的画上。

738
00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:41.840
People look a little scared.
大家看起来有点害怕。

739
00:29:42.340 --> 00:29:45.100
Pam looks to Jim like, what the F?
帕姆看着吉姆，那表情仿佛在说，搞什么鬼？

740
00:29:45.580 --> 00:29:49.480
Dwight smiles smugly at Pam like, that's what you get.
德怀特得意地冲帕姆笑了笑，仿佛在说，这就是你的下场。

741
00:29:50.040 --> 00:29:52.040
That was all in the script.
这些全都在剧本里。

742
00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:52.500
Yeah.
没错。

743
00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:55.740
And I remember us rehearsing it, Jenna.
我还记得我们排练的时候，珍娜。

744
00:29:56.180 --> 00:30:02.160
We rehearsed all of this because it was like a one-act play, and Kathy had to walk around the room and engage with all of us.
我们排练了所有这些，因为它就像一出独幕剧，凯西必须在房间里走来走去，与我们每个人互动。

745
00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:07.800
I thought it was great when I rewatched it because you really feel her presence amongst all the workers.
重看的时候我觉得这很棒，因为你真的能感受到她在所有员工中的存在感。

746
00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:10.600
The way they staged her walking through the bullpen.
他们安排她穿过办公区的走位方式。

747
00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:12.620
But here's what I wanted to share.
但这就是我想分享的。

748
00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:13.540
Here's my tidbit.
这是我的小花絮。

749
00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:19.560
If you go to three minutes, six seconds, that's when Joe crosses to Angela's desk for the glue stick.
如果你看到三分零六秒，那就是乔走到安吉拉桌前拿胶棒的时候。

750
00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:29.280
And this was the moment in rehearsal when Kathy Bates walked over to my desk, and I was really cold, and I had my heater cranked, and it had been cranked all morning.
这就是排练时凯西·贝茨走到我桌子前的那一刻，当时我真的很冷，我把取暖器开到了最大，而且整个上午都开着。

751
00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:33.180
And she said, it's like the Sahara over here.
她说，这儿简直像撒哈拉沙漠一样。

752
00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:35.240
And I'll never forget it.
我永远都不会忘记。

753
00:30:35.300 --> 00:30:37.620
I wrote about it in our book, The Office BFF.
我在我们的书《办公室闺蜜》里写过这件事。

754
00:30:38.260 --> 00:30:43.000
Because I, with my foot, went under the desk to try to turn my heater off.
因为我当时想用脚伸到桌子底下把加热器关掉。

755
00:30:43.320 --> 00:30:44.680
And I was, like, so embarrassed.
我当时尴尬极了。

756
00:30:44.820 --> 00:30:48.500
I was like, oh, no, Kathy Bates, like, my desk area is so hot.
我心想，天哪，凯西·贝茨，我的办公桌区域太热了。

757
00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:51.360
As soon as I rewatched it, I was like, that was the time.
我重温那一集时，心想，就是那次了。

758
00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:53.660
Yeah, I wondered if that was the time.
是啊，我也在想是不是那次。

759
00:30:53.820 --> 00:30:54.320
That's it.
就是那次。

760
00:30:55.520 --> 00:31:02.200
Well, at the end of all of this, Michael is trying to tell Joe, listen, nobody in our office spoke to the press.
总之，在这一切的最后，迈克尔试图告诉乔，听着，我们办公室没人向媒体透露过消息。

761
00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:04.120
It wasn't anyone here.
不是我们这儿的任何人。

762
00:31:04.840 --> 00:31:07.420
But, of course, Phyllis knows.
但当然，菲利斯知道。

763
00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:08.600
That it was Andy.
知道是安迪干的。

764
00:31:08.860 --> 00:31:09.200
Yeah.
没错。

765
00:31:09.520 --> 00:31:14.020
And she's kind of like, Andy, you better say it was you.
她当时就像是在说，安迪，你最好承认是你干的。

766
00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:14.640
Come on.
拜托。

767
00:31:15.380 --> 00:31:17.220
And Andy threatens her.
然后安迪威胁了她。

768
00:31:17.420 --> 00:31:19.440
With such, like, intensity.
语气还特别凶狠。

769
00:31:19.780 --> 00:31:20.480
I know.
我知道。

770
00:31:20.760 --> 00:31:24.680
He said he will break her glasses, right, and poke them in her eyeball or something?
他说他要弄碎她的眼镜，对吧，然后把碎片戳进她的眼球里还是什么的？

771
00:31:24.820 --> 00:31:25.240
What did he say?
他到底说了什么？

772
00:31:25.240 --> 00:31:28.560
Yeah, you know, the little nubs of your glasses that sit on your nose?
对，你知道眼镜架在鼻梁上的那个小鼻托吗？

773
00:31:28.780 --> 00:31:32.440
There's a name for them, and he says he's going to break them off and poke them in her eyes.
那东西有个名字，他说他要把它们掰下来戳进她的眼睛里。

774
00:31:33.740 --> 00:31:34.420
That's hardcore.
那也太狠了。

775
00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:40.760
Well, he has a talking head where he explains that he is no whistleblower.
嗯，他有个采访镜头，解释说他绝不是告密者。

776
00:31:40.760 --> 00:31:47.700
In fact, generations of Bernards have made their money by silencing whistleblowers.
事实上，伯纳德家族几代人都是靠让告密者闭嘴来赚钱的。

777
00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:51.080
In fact, Woody Guthrie wrote a song about them.
事实上，伍迪·格思里还为他们写过一首歌。

778
00:31:51.320 --> 00:31:58.800
For those of you who don't know who Woody Guthrie is, he was, by all accounts on the internet and people who love folk music,
对于那些不知道伍迪·格思里是谁的人，据互联网和所有热爱民谣音乐的人所说，

779
00:31:59.140 --> 00:32:02.260
one of the most significant figures in American folk music.
他是美国民谣音乐界最重要的人物之一。

780
00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:04.920
He was, quote, a poet of the people.
他被称为“人民的诗人”。

781
00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:09.560
His songs captured the heart of hard economic times and war.
他的歌捕捉到了艰难经济时期和战争中的人心。

782
00:32:09.880 --> 00:32:13.380
One of his songs you might all know is This Land Is Your Land.
你们可能都听过他的一首歌，叫《这片土地是你的土地》。

783
00:32:14.420 --> 00:32:16.000
This land is your land.
这片土地是你的土地。

784
00:32:16.140 --> 00:32:17.260
This land is my land.
这片土地是我的土地。

785
00:32:17.360 --> 00:32:17.840
You know that one.
你们知道这首歌吧。

786
00:32:18.880 --> 00:32:21.580
And incidentally, he was born today.
顺便说一句，他就是今天出生的。

787
00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:23.100
What?
什么？

788
00:32:23.320 --> 00:32:24.260
In 1912.
1912年。

789
00:32:24.260 --> 00:32:27.540
He was born on July 14th, which is the day we're recording this.
他出生于7月14日，也就是我们录制这一集的日子。

790
00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:28.680
That is crazy.
太不可思议了。

791
00:32:28.880 --> 00:32:29.320
I know.
我知道。

792
00:32:29.420 --> 00:32:30.220
I thought so, too.
我也这么觉得。

793
00:32:30.220 --> 00:32:33.560
We got some mail from Barrett G.
我们收到了来自阿肯色州巴雷特·G的来信，

794
00:32:33.820 --> 00:32:35.780
in Arkansas who said,
他说，

795
00:32:36.620 --> 00:32:44.360
Have y'all heard the awesome song someone made from that snippet that Andy sang about his family being whistleblowers?
你们听过有人把安迪唱的那段关于他家族是告密者的片段做成的超棒歌曲吗？

796
00:32:44.740 --> 00:32:46.180
It's actually really good.
真的非常好听。

797
00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:47.500
No, I haven't.
没有，我没听过。

798
00:32:47.640 --> 00:32:48.680
I found it.
我找到了。

799
00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:52.380
It's by someone named Stormlight on SoundCloud.
是SoundCloud上一个叫Stormlight的人做的。

800
00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:54.080
Let's hear it.
我们来听听看。

801
00:32:54.760 --> 00:33:05.980
From ashes they rose up, from nothing they came.
他们从灰烬中崛起，从虚无中走来。

802
00:33:07.540 --> 00:33:11.860
From utter damnation, Bernard was their name.
从彻底的诅咒中，他们以伯纳德为名。

803
00:33:13.780 --> 00:33:17.620
The shame of their past drove them near the insane.
过去的耻辱让他们几近疯狂。

804
00:33:19.500 --> 00:33:23.380
When all of it crumbled while others they blamed.
当一切崩塌，而他们却在指责他人。

805
00:33:26.880 --> 00:33:30.140
Desperate for money, but with nothing to show.
渴望金钱，却一无所有。

806
00:33:32.260 --> 00:33:36.040
They stooped to the lowest a person could go.
他们堕落到了人所能及的最低点。

807
00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:42.100
Well, who are the ones who will tell what they know?
那么，谁会说出他们所知道的一切呢？

808
